-- What Is Copywriting? Content Writing Tutorial For Beginners
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Tell copywriting apart from content writing and know when each is wanted', 'Describe what a client is actually buying when they buy words', 'Recognise the kinds of writing that pay and the kinds that do not'],
  notes = 'COPYWRITING is writing that asks someone to act: buy, enquire, sign up, come in. CONTENT WRITING is writing that informs or helps, building trust so that someone eventually acts. Both are paid work, they need different instincts, and a client will use the words interchangeably — so listen for what they want the reader to DO.

WHAT A CLIENT IS ACTUALLY BUYING. Not sentences. They are buying the result the sentences produce: more enquiries, fewer confused customers, a product that sells itself on the page. This is why the writer who understands the business outsells the writer with the better vocabulary, every time. Your prose is the delivery mechanism, not the product.

WHERE THE WORK IS, honestly ranked by how easy it is to get paid for: product descriptions and listings; website pages for small businesses; email and message sequences; social captions; blog and article writing; and sales pages, which pay most and require the most skill.

WHAT DOES NOT PAY WELL, and you should know before investing months: writing for content mills at a fraction of a shilling a word; ''exposure'' work; and anything where you are paid for volume rather than result. Volume writing is also the part most exposed to automation, which is worth thinking about now rather than in two years.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — the market question a Kenyan writer has to answer early: are you writing for LOCAL clients or INTERNATIONAL ones? They are different jobs. Local clients pay in shillings with no transfer cost, brief you by phone, and want copy for a market you actually understand — which is a genuine advantage no foreign writer has. International clients pay more per piece, expect a particular register of English, and bring transfer costs and time-zone gaps. Neither is better. Choose deliberately rather than drifting, because the samples, the rates and the positioning are different for each.',
  practice_activity = 'Find five examples of paid writing you encounter in one day - a product listing, a poster, a sales email, a company page, a social caption. For each, write down what the writer wanted the reader to do, and whether they succeeded on you. Then pick which market you are aiming at, local or international, and write one sentence on why.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What is a client actually buying when they pay for copy?", "options": ["The result the words produce - enquiries, sales, fewer confused customers", "Well-constructed sentences", "A certain number of words", "Industry vocabulary"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What advantage does a Kenyan writer have with local clients?", "options": ["Understanding a market no foreign writer knows, with no transfer cost or time-zone gap", "Lower rates than competitors", "Faster turnaround times", "Access to more platforms"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '7047cbe8-a07b-437f-9804-295e044dbfa7';

-- Basics of Copywriting
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Write about what the reader gets rather than what the product is', 'Use a simple structure that carries a reader to an action', 'Apply proof so a claim is believed rather than merely stated'],
  notes = 'The fundamentals, which are few and which almost nobody applies consistently.

FEATURES VERSUS BENEFITS. A feature is what it is; a benefit is what it does for the reader. ''5000mAh battery'' is a feature. ''Lasts the whole day without hunting for a socket'' is the benefit. Write benefits, and use features as the evidence for them. This one distinction separates copy that works from copy that describes.

THE READER''S ONLY QUESTION is ''what is in this for me''. Count how many sentences in a piece of copy are about the company — we are excited, we were founded in, we pride ourselves — and rewrite each one as something the reader receives.

A STRUCTURE THAT WORKS: name the problem the reader already has; show you understand it; present the thing as the answer; prove it; say exactly what to do next. That skeleton fits a product listing, an email and a sales page alike, and it is a starting point rather than a cage.

PROOF IS WHAT MAKES A CLAIM BELIEVABLE. Numbers, customer words, photographs of real work, a guarantee, a named reference. ''Best in Kenya'' is noise; ''340 deliveries last month, no breakages'' is proof. Beginners write more adjectives when what the copy needs is more evidence.

ONE CLEAR NEXT STEP. Not four options. Call this number, send a message, come to the shop.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — a caution about imported persuasion. Much copywriting advice comes from a market that rewards heavy pressure: countdown timers, exaggerated promises, manufactured scarcity. That register reads as untrustworthy to many Kenyan readers, and in a market where most buying decisions run partly on personal reputation, being caught overpromising is expensive and durable. Specific, honest and confident beats loud. Price stated plainly, delivery actually explained, and a real guarantee will outperform urgency tricks with a local audience — and it is also the version you can defend when the customer arrives.',
  practice_activity = 'Take any product and write ten features, then convert each into the benefit a reader actually cares about. Then write a short piece of copy using the five-part structure, and count your sentences: mark each one as about the company or about the reader. Rewrite until most are about the reader, and replace every adjective you can with a piece of evidence.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What is the difference between a feature and a benefit?", "options": ["A feature is what it is; a benefit is what the reader gets from it", "A benefit is longer and more descriptive", "Features are technical, benefits are emotional", "There is no practical difference"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Why does heavy urgency and pressure often backfire with a local audience?", "options": ["It reads as untrustworthy where buying runs partly on personal reputation", "It is prohibited by advertising rules", "It takes more words", "It only works in written form"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '6bfbe445-a6fd-44cd-a991-fa3725e49688';

-- How to Write Headlines for Beginner Copywriters
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Write a headline that earns the next line', 'Use specificity instead of cleverness', 'Produce many options and choose rather than settling for the first'],
  notes = 'The headline decides whether anything else gets read. It is the highest-leverage sentence you will write and it deserves a disproportionate share of your time.

ITS ONE JOB is to get the next line read. Not to summarise, not to be witty, not to include the company name. Just that.

SPECIFIC BEATS CLEVER, reliably. ''Save Money on Transport'' is nothing. ''Cut your monthly matatu spend by choosing routes at the right time'' is a headline, because it makes a definite promise to a definite person. Cleverness is the commonest beginner trap — a pun that makes the writer smile costs the reader the two seconds in which they decide to leave.

WHAT MAKES ONE WORK: a clear benefit, a number, a named audience, a question the reader is already asking, or something genuinely surprising. Usually two of those together.

WRITE TWENTY. The first five are obvious, the next ten are exploration, and the good one usually appears after that. Professionals do this as a matter of course; beginners write one and start polishing the paragraph beneath it.

THE HEADLINE MUST MATCH WHAT FOLLOWS. A promise the page does not deliver produces someone who leaves feeling tricked, and in a small market that person tells others.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — two things for this audience. First, a headline is usually read on a phone, often truncated, so put the important words at the FRONT: the reader may only see the first few. Second, language register matters more than the templates admit. ''Skyrocket your revenue'' is a phrasing borrowed from another market and lands as foreign or salesy here. Write the headline in the way a person in your reader''s position would actually say it — including mixing English and Kiswahili where the audience does — and it will be trusted more, which is what the headline is really buying.',
  practice_activity = 'Take one real product or service and write twenty headlines for it without stopping to judge. Then pick the best three and test them: read each aloud, check the important words come first, and ask someone which one makes them want to hear more. Finally check that whatever you promise is actually delivered by the copy underneath.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What is the headline''s only job?", "options": ["To get the next line read", "To summarise the whole piece", "To include the business name", "To be memorable"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Why should the important words come first in a headline?", "options": ["It will be read on a phone and often truncated, so the reader may only see the start", "Search engines weight the first words", "It reads faster aloud", "Headlines are limited to five words"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '29fe9018-da2c-47b0-ac0c-00b148dc2bde';

-- How to Write Meaningful Body Copy
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Keep a reader moving from one line to the next', 'Answer objections inside the copy rather than hoping they do not arise', 'Cut a draft to what actually earns its place'],
  notes = 'Module 4 covered how people read on a screen and why plain language wins. This lesson is about what goes in the paragraphs.

EVERY LINE EXISTS TO EARN THE NEXT ONE. That is the whole mechanic. Short opening sentences, one idea per paragraph, and a reason to continue at the end of each. When a reader stops, they stop at a line that gave them nothing.

ANSWER THE OBJECTIONS IN THE COPY. Every reader has three or four unspoken reasons not to act: too expensive, will not work for me, do not trust you, not right now. Name each one and answer it. Copy that ignores objections leaves the reader to resolve them alone, and alone they resolve them as no.

WRITE THE FIRST DRAFT FAST AND BADLY. The draft is for getting the argument down. The work is in the second pass.

THEN CUT. Most first drafts lose a third with no loss of meaning: every ''in order to'', every ''very'', every sentence restating the last one, every paragraph that was you thinking rather than telling. Cutting is where copy actually gets good, and it is the step beginners skip because deleting their own writing feels like waste.

READ IT ALOUD. Anywhere you stumble is a place the reader will stumble. This is the single cheapest editing tool there is.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — for this market, two objections deserve permanent space in almost any copy, because they are what actually stops the sale. TRUST: is this business real, will the thing arrive, what happens if it does not? Answer it with a physical location, a named person, real photographs, and a plain refund or replacement promise. And COST OF GETTING IT: delivery charges, how far the shop is, whether payment is on delivery. A price that looks good and then collects a surprise delivery fee loses both the sale and the reputation. Say the whole cost plainly in the copy.',
  practice_activity = 'Take a draft you have written and do three passes. First, list the four reasons a reader would not act and make sure each is answered in the text. Second, cut a third of the words without losing meaning. Third, read it aloud and fix every place you stumble. Compare the start and end versions.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What is the mechanic that keeps someone reading?", "options": ["Every line exists to earn the next one", "Varying the sentence length", "Using subheadings throughout", "Including numbers regularly"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Which two objections deserve space in almost any local copy?", "options": ["Whether the business is real and trustworthy, and the full cost including delivery", "The brand history and the company values", "The competitors and their prices", "The payment platform and the opening hours"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '5b17bf35-a4f0-47ec-a175-e66e473380b6';

-- How to Use Storytelling in Copywriting
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Use a short story to make a claim believable', 'Structure a story so it arrives at the point', 'Source real stories instead of inventing them'],
  notes = 'A story works because it is concrete. ''Our service saves time'' is a claim the reader must accept or reject. ''Mary used to close the shop for two hours every Thursday to go to the bank'' is a picture, and the reader arrives at the conclusion themselves — which is the only conclusion anyone truly believes.

A SHORT STRUCTURE THAT WORKS: a specific person in a specific situation; the problem, concretely; what they tried that did not work; the change; where they are now. Four or five sentences is usually enough. Copy does not need a long story, it needs a true one.

BE SPECIFIC. A name, a place, a number, a detail. ''A customer in Nakuru'' beats ''our clients''. The detail is what makes it believable, and vagueness is what makes a testimonial read as invented.

GET REAL STORIES. Ask the client for three customers you may speak to. Ten minutes on the phone produces better material than an afternoon of imagining, and the phrases customers use become the copy — they describe the benefit better than you will.

NEVER INVENT A CUSTOMER. Fabricated testimonials are dishonest, they are recognisable, and in a market where reputation travels personally, being caught ends a business. If there are no customers yet, say so honestly and use a different form of proof: the owner''s own story, the process, a guarantee.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — the strongest story available to most small businesses here is the OWNER''S. Why they started, what they were doing before, what they fix for people now. It is true, nobody else has it, and it answers the trust question from the last lesson before it is asked. It also travels: a founder''s story gets repeated by customers in a way that product copy never does. Ask for it in the brief, and get permission before publishing anything about a named customer.',
  practice_activity = 'Interview one real person about a product or service they use - ten minutes, recorded or noted. Write down the exact phrases they use. Then write a five-sentence story following the structure, using their words rather than yours. Also write the owner''s story for one business you know, in under a hundred words.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Why does a story work better than a claim?", "options": ["It is concrete, so the reader reaches the conclusion themselves", "It is longer and holds attention", "It avoids mentioning the product", "Readers prefer narrative to fact"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "There are no customer stories yet. What do you do?", "options": ["Say so honestly and use other proof - the owner''s story, the process, a guarantee", "Write a plausible example customer", "Use a competitor''s testimonial as a template", "Leave the proof section out"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'ea3ad2eb-5549-43cf-b2fc-17b276b8a2c7';

-- Writing Copy for Ads, Emails, and Landing Pages
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Adjust length and structure to the format', 'Write a subject line and an opening that survive a crowded inbox', 'Keep a landing page focused on one action'],
  notes = 'Same principles, different shapes. The format decides how much room you have and how much attention you start with.

ADS. Very little room, cold audience. One benefit, stated plainly, with one action. The first line does the work — everything from the headline lesson applies at double strength. Resist listing three benefits in four lines; you will communicate none.

EMAILS. A warm audience who gave permission, which is a privilege and easy to lose. The SUBJECT LINE decides whether it is opened: short, specific, no false promises, nothing that reads like a trick. The FIRST LINE is often visible in the preview, so it is a second headline rather than ''I hope this email finds you well''. Then one idea, one action. And ask yourself before sending: would a reasonable person find this worth receiving? Sending regularly to people who do not want it does not build a list, it burns one.

LANDING PAGES. One page, one action, and nothing else on it. No menu of other options, no alternative offers. The whole page argues for a single thing: promise at the top, proof in the middle, the action repeated where a reader is likely to be convinced. Every additional choice reduces the chance of the one you want.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — two formats no international course lists and both of which you will be paid for here. WHATSAPP MESSAGES, which are the real sales channel for many businesses: the first line shows in the notification, so it carries the whole weight; keep it short enough to read without expanding; and write it as a person speaking, not as a broadcast. And SMS, which is still heavily used, is unforgiving at its length limit and forces genuinely disciplined writing. Practise both. A writer who can produce a message that gets a reply on WhatsApp is more useful to a Kenyan business than one who can write a long landing page.',
  practice_activity = 'Take one offer and write it four ways: an advert of under thirty words, an email with a subject line and a first line that work in preview, a landing page outline with one action, and a WhatsApp message whose first line stands alone in a notification. Send the WhatsApp version to a willing friend and see whether they reply without being prompted.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What makes a landing page work?", "options": ["One page, one action, with every other choice removed", "A complete menu so visitors can explore", "Several offers to suit different readers", "A long page covering every detail"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What carries the weight in a WhatsApp message?", "options": ["The first line, since it shows in the notification", "The closing call to action", "The length of the message", "The images attached"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '9c4c5dd0-56f1-47a3-8be0-bc6c43d125df';
